1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
Imagine a job where you work only at

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:03,280
night...

3
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:05,910
because the law doesn't want anyone to see

4
00:00:05,910 --> 00:00:06,610
you.

5
00:00:06,870 --> 00:00:08,830
Or a job where the king's most powerful

6
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:11,370
servant... was the man who helped him on

7
00:00:11,370 --> 00:00:12,210
the toilet.

8
00:00:12,610 --> 00:00:14,710
Or a job where your boss paid you

9
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:16,510
to swallow a toad.

10
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:18,930
These were real jobs in old England.

11
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:22,110
Tonight, we rank eight of the worst, from

12
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:24,130
bad... to unbelievable.

13
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:26,390
And number one is a job you will

14
00:00:26,390 --> 00:00:28,330
never, ever want.

15
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:33,510
Number eight: the ale conner.

16
00:00:33,910 --> 00:00:35,630
At first, this sounds like the best job

17
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:36,330
in town.

18
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:38,610
In medieval England, an ale conner was

19
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:40,430
chosen by the local court to walk from

20
00:00:40,430 --> 00:00:42,590
alehouse to alehouse...

21
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:44,000
and taste the ale.

22
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:46,540
If a brewer sold weak ale, short measures,

23
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:48,340
or charged too much...

24
00:00:48,910 --> 00:00:50,450
the ale conner could drag them before the

25
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:51,670
court to be fined.

26
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:53,690
So why is it on this list?

27
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:56,220
Because the people you tested were your

28
00:00:56,220 --> 00:00:58,320
neighbours. And nobody likes the person

29
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,100
who reports them.

30
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:01,400
You may have heard that ale conners sat

31
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,160
in a puddle of ale in leather trousers,

32
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:04,880
to see if they stuck to the bench.

33
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:07,180
Beer historians say that story is almost

34
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:08,120
certainly a myth.

35
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:10,100
But some towns still keep the ceremony

36
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:11,900
alive today... just for fun.

37
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:17,440
Number seven: the Groom of the Stool.

38
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,060
In the Tudor court, the "stool" was the

39
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:20,840
king's toilet.

40
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:23,240
And the groom was the servant who looked

41
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,960
after the king while he used it.

42
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:27,240
Sounds terrible. But here's the twist.

43
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:29,340
Because he was alone with the king every

44
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:31,280
day, the groom became one of the most

45
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:32,980
trusted men in the country.

46
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:35,400
Under Henry the Eighth, the groom ran the

47
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:37,460
king's private rooms, handled his money,

48
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:38,920
and heard his secrets.

49
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:40,840
Nobles fought for this job.

50
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:42,800
But being that close to Henry was

51
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:43,500
dangerous.

52
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:47,200
In 1536, his groom, Sir Henry Norris, was

53
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:48,840
accused of a romance with Queen Anne

54
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:49,540
Boleyn.

55
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:51,820
The charges were almost certainly false.

56
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:53,660
He was executed anyway.

57
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:58,920
Number six: the toad eater.

58
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:02,060
In the 1600s, travelling quacks sold fake

59
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:03,500
medicine at fairs.

60
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:05,140
And many brought an assistant.

61
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:07,640
People believed every toad was deadly

62
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:08,340
poisonous.

63
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:10,740
So, the story goes, the assistant would

64
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:13,280
swallow a toad in front of the crowd...

65
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:16,350
pretend to fall down, twisting in pain...

66
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:18,320
and then the quack would give him a

67
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:19,820
sip of "miracle medicine".

68
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:21,340
He'd jump up, cured!

69
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:23,400
And the crowd would rush to buy a

70
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,100
bottle.

71
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:25,720
This job left its mark on our language.

72
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:28,220
A person who flatters someone powerful

73
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:29,860
became a "toad-eater"...

74
00:02:30,430 --> 00:02:31,750
and later, a "toady".

75
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:32,950
A word we still use today.

76
00:02:35,670 --> 00:02:38,050
Number five: the sin eater.

77
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:40,230
This one is spooky.

78
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:43,150
In the 1680s, a writer named John Aubrey

79
00:02:43,150 --> 00:02:45,270
described a custom in parts of Wales and

80
00:02:45,270 --> 00:02:46,070
the English border.

81
00:02:46,470 --> 00:02:48,590
When someone died, a poor person was hired

82
00:02:48,590 --> 00:02:49,390
to come to the house.

83
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:51,410
Bread and a bowl of ale were passed

84
00:02:51,410 --> 00:02:52,710
over the body...

85
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:55,040
and the sin eater ate and drank them,

86
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:56,760
taking on the dead person's sins.

87
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:58,780
The family believed this let the soul rest

88
00:02:58,780 --> 00:02:59,480
in peace.

89
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:01,420
The sin eater got a few coins...

90
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,040
and was then avoided by everyone.

91
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:05,200
Historians still argue about how common

92
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:06,200
this really was.

93
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,060
But the idea of a job where you

94
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:09,520
swallow other people's sins...

95
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,000
is hard to forget.

96
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:16,100
Number four: the saltpetre man.

97
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:18,020
To make gunpowder, England needed

98
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:18,750
saltpetre.

99
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:20,450
And one of the best places to find

100
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:22,750
it... was soil soaked with animal waste.

101
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:25,350
Like the floor of your stable, your pigeon

102
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:26,630
house, or your cellar.

103
00:03:27,030 --> 00:03:28,750
So the crown gave saltpetre men the right

104
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:29,450
to dig.

105
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:32,130
In 1627, a royal order even let them

106
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:33,890
dig up the floors of stables and

107
00:03:33,890 --> 00:03:34,790
cellars...

108
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:37,020
and told owners to leave the floors as

109
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:38,860
loose earth, so they'd be easier to dig

110
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:39,640
next time.

111
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,280
Imagine a stranger arriving with a shovel,

112
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:44,120
and you're not allowed to say no.

113
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:45,880
Saltpetre men were some of the most hated

114
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:46,880
people in the country.

115
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:51,300
Number three: the fuller.

116
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:53,960
Fresh wool cloth is greasy and loose.

117
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:56,500
It has to be cleaned and thickened.

118
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:58,880
For centuries, one way to do it was

119
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:00,480
with stale urine.

120
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:02,820
Collected, left to stand for days, and

121
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:04,860
poured into a big tub with the cloth.

122
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:07,520
Then the fuller climbed in...

123
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:09,280
and stamped on the cloth with his bare

124
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:11,360
feet. For hours.

125
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:13,700
The ammonia in old urine helped strip the

126
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:15,100
grease and whiten the wool.

127
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:17,220
Over time, water-powered fulling mills

128
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:18,660
took over much of the stamping.

129
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:20,540
But the job left a mark too.

130
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,600
Fuller, Tucker and Walker are all English

131
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:25,160
surnames that come from this work.

132
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:30,180
Number two: the tanner.

133
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:32,280
Turning animal skins into leather was one

134
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:34,100
of the smelliest jobs in history.

135
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:36,580
Skins were scraped, soaked for months in

136
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:38,700
pits, and softened with a paste made of

137
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:39,400
dung.

138
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:40,820
Dog dung and pigeon droppings were

139
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:41,520
favourites.

140
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:43,800
The smell was so bad that towns often

141
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:46,060
pushed tanners outside the walls, next to

142
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:46,760
a river.

143
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:49,420
Around 1425, the brewers of Colchester

144
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:51,060
complained that the tanners were soaking

145
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:52,860
hides in the river they used for brewing.

146
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:56,280
Leather was everywhere: shoes, belts,

147
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:59,060
buckets, saddles. Someone had to make it.

148
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:01,420
But almost nobody wanted to live next

149
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:01,800
door.

150
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:06,400
And number one...

151
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:07,480
the gong farmer.

152
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:09,120
A "gong" was a toilet.

153
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:11,360
And a gong farmer emptied the pits

154
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:12,520
underneath them.

155
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:15,920
By hand. With a shovel and a bucket.

156
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:18,280
They were usually allowed to work only at

157
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:20,020
night, so the waste they carried became

158
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:21,180
known as "night soil".

159
00:05:21,580 --> 00:05:23,520
The pits had no fresh air, and the

160
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:25,360
fumes could knock a worker out.

161
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:28,580
In 1326, a Londoner known as Richard the

162
00:05:28,580 --> 00:05:30,780
Raker fell through the rotten boards of

163
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:32,980
his toilet... and drowned in the pit

164
00:05:32,980 --> 00:05:33,680
below.

165
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,580
So why would anyone take this job?

166
00:05:36,230 --> 00:05:39,090
Money. Gong farmers were paid far more

167
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:40,070
than most workers.

168
00:05:40,470 --> 00:05:42,270
Because some jobs pay well...

169
00:05:42,270 --> 00:05:44,310
exactly because nobody else will do them.

170
00:05:44,710 --> 00:05:45,770
So which job would you pick?

171
00:05:46,190 --> 00:05:46,970
Tell us in the comments.

172
00:05:47,370 --> 00:05:49,250
And if you want more true whys, you

173
00:05:49,250 --> 00:05:49,950
know what to do.

174
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:50,890
See you next time.
